  "details": "When an avatar contact doesn’t react, there’s currently no easy way to find out why. The output log doesn’t record who touched a contact, or whether that person was allowed to, so creators and users are left guessing whether the cause is the avatar setup, a safety setting, or a permission.\n\nRequest: when a contact on the local player’s avatar is touched, write a log line that includes:\n\n- The name and user ID of the player who touched it\n\n- The contact receiver’s name\n\n- Whether that player had permission to trigger it, and if not, which setting blocked it\n\nWhy this helps:\n\n- Troubleshooting: users can see straight away whether a contact was blocked or never touched at all.\n\n- Creators: it’s much easier to support people using their avatars when “it doesn’t work” can be checked in a log.\n\n- OSC and companion apps: tools that read the log could tell who an interaction was with. Today they can only guess from who’s in the instance.\n\nKeeping it manageable: contacts can fire many times a second, so one line when a touch starts would be enough. This could sit behind a debug logging option, or only cover the local player’s own avatar, to keep logs small and limit what’s recorded about other players.\n\nExample (the format is only a suggestion, styled after VRChat’s existing log lines, not something VRChat logs today):\n\n```\n\n2026.11.03 21:14:02 Debug      -  [AvatarDynamics] Contact touched: Head_Pat by Ember Fox (usr_bbbbbbbb-1111-2222-3333-444444444444) | Allowed: true\n\n2026.11.03 21:14:37 Debug      -  [AvatarDynamics] Contact touched: Head_Pat by Grey (usr_cccccccc-1111-2222-3333-444444444444) | Allowed: false (blocked by: contact permissions)\n\n```",
